# Prompt: Stage geometry & Void update

You are working in the `hiyroscript/alva` repo. Read `CLAUDE.md`, `UPDATES.md`, `ALVA_SPEC.md` and `docs/README.md` first and follow every rule in them (the character system, the protected `character_rule`, controller and touch comfort, never special-casing a fighter id in shared code). Run `node --test` before you start, so you know the baseline, and again at the end. This work is a new named update — call it the **Void update** — and gets its own entry in `UPDATES.md`.

## What exists today (verified in the code)

- Stage geometry is in `js/data/maps.js`. Each stage has a `*_MAIN` rectangle (`mainStage`) and a `*_VOID` built with `voidAround(main, { side, top, bottom })`: `side` = units past each ledge, `top` = units above the main stage's top, `bottom` = units below the main stage's top. `cameraBounds` is `cameraAround(VOID, 140)`. Practice Ground has its own in `js/data/practice-map.js`.
- Current values:

| Stage | Main left–right | Main length | Void side | Void top | Void bottom |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Desert | 1120–2480 | **1360** | 360 | **760** | 400 |
| City | 1080–2520 | **1440** | **340** | 800 | **420** |
| Asperitas | 1100–2500 | 1400 | 360 | **840** | 400 |
| Adamaris (continuous ground) | 1100–2500 | 1400 (combat area) | 360 | 800 | 400 |
| Jökull (3 sections 260/880/260) | 1100–2500 | 1400 | 360 | 800 | **420** |
| Practice Ground | 1360–2640 | **1280** | **380** | **760** | 400 |

  So they do **not** match today. The most common values are main length 1400 and margins side 360 / top 800 / bottom 400.
- Overtime: `Battle.update` (`js/game/battle.js`) calls `setVoidPressure(this.overtimeProgress)` each step, where `overtimeProgress` is **linear** in overtime time. `Arena.setVoidPressure` (`js/game/arena.js`) calls `StageCollision.closeVoid(progress, CONFIG.battle.overtimeVoid)` (`js/game/physics.js`), which moves the sides linearly toward `sideEndGap` (120) past each ledge and the bottom toward `bottomEndGap` (140) below the top; the top never moves; `MIN_VOID_GAP` 40 is the floor. `Arena.voidWaveSpeed` already rises as `1 + (max − 1)·p²` with `maxWaveSpeedMultiplier` 4. Config is `CONFIG.battle.overtimeVoid` in `js/config.js` (`overtimeSeconds` 60).
- Match end: `Battle.finishMatch` / `liveResult` decide the loser by points, then by Launch Point at time-out (`reason` `time`, `points`, `overtimePoints`, `overtimeLaunchPoint`, `void`, or a draw). Only a Void fall (`Arena.checkVoid`) plays the elimination burst (`HitEffects` in `js/game/rendering/hit-fx.js`, the burst in the fighter's `eliminationPalette`, plus `addVoidRipple`) and takes the fighter out (`lostToVoid`, `detachFromPlay`). A loser decided by the clock stays standing on screen.
- The Launch Point fire: `LAUNCH_POINT_FIRE` 60 / `_OUT` 55 and `LAUNCH_POINT_INFERNO` 100 / `_OUT` 95 in `js/ui/hud.js`. The comment there explains they were picked by simulated launches on Yerma against today's Void distances (`LAUNCH_UNIT_SPEED` 7 in `js/data/launch.js`, Base Launch 2/3, 3-damage hit, fighter not recovering). `launchIsLethal` in `hit-fx.js` reads `stage.void` live.
- The CPU reads the Void live through `js/game/ai/cpu-intelligence/stage-model.js` (`stage.void`), with judgement in `values.js`, `situation.js`, `prediction.js`, `strategy.js`, `defense.js`. There is a benchmark at `tests/benchmarks/cpu-benchmark.mjs`.
- Each stage theme (`js/stages/*-theme.js`) paints scenery out to `m.cameraBounds`; the drawn Void is `stage-theme.js` `drawVoid`.
- Tests that touch this: `tests/integration/overtime.test.mjs`, `match-score.test.mjs`, `practice-ground.test.mjs`, `tests/systems/platform-stage.test.mjs`, `jokull.test.mjs`, `adamaris.test.mjs`, `void-ripple.test.mjs`, `launch-bounce.test.mjs`, `stage-hazards.test.mjs`, `platform-hazards.test.mjs`, `tests/interface/battle-screen.test.mjs`.

## Tasks, in order

### 1. One main-stage length everywhere

Make every finite main stage exactly **1400** units long (`right − left`). Today that means fixing **Desert** (1360) and **City** (1440), and **Practice Ground** (1280). Adamaris's continuous ground is excluded from the length rule (its `mainStage` is already 1400 as its combat area; leave it).

- Keep each stage centred where it is (centre x 1800, Practice 2000) unless that breaks something; change `left`/`right`, not `top`/`bottom`.
- Move whatever sits on or relative to the main stage so it still makes sense: spawns, platforms (City's racks at its ledges, `bulkhead` solid), Desert's rock outcrops, hazard envelopes (Asperitas clouds must stay disjoint from the main stage — the build check enforces it), Jökull's sections must still tile the main stage exactly with the centre one unbreakable.
- Check the theme art that is drawn to the main stage's edges (cliff faces, slab ends, rubble, ledge details) still lines up.
- Put the length in one shared constant in `maps.js` (e.g. `MAIN_STAGE_LENGTH = 1400`) and add a test that asserts every finite stage, Practice Ground included, uses it.

### 2. One Void-free space everywhere

Make the Void's margins identical on **every** stage, Adamaris and Practice Ground included: side **360**, top **800**, bottom **400** (the most common values today). Fix Desert (top 760), City (side 340, bottom 420), Asperitas (top 840), Jökull (bottom 420), Practice (side 380, top 760). Define the margins once in `maps.js` (e.g. `VOID_MARGINS`) and have every stage use it rather than its own literals. Add a test that the Void-free rectangle (Void minus main stage, measured as margins) is the same on every stage.

Before moving on, confirm Asperitas's top margin change is still safe: its comment says no jump from its highest cloud reaches the top line — re-check that with the new number.

### 3. Push the Void back 1.3×

Once step 2 is in, multiply the **side** and **top** margins by 1.3 and leave the **bottom** alone:

- side 360 → **468** past each ledge
- top 800 → **1040** above the main stage's top
- bottom stays **400**

(1.3× applies to the margin — the distance from the ledge / from the stage top — not to the whole rectangle from the stage centre.) This must still be the one shared value, so every stage gets the same pushed-back Void. Update the tests from step 2 to the new numbers.

Consequences to handle:

- `cameraBounds` grows with it (it is derived). Check the camera still frames the fight well at the new size on desktop and phone landscape (`js/game/rendering/camera.js`, the camera & mobile layout update in `UPDATES.md`); don't let the default framing zoom out noticeably during normal play.
- **Stage art.** Every theme paints out to `cameraBounds`, so now there is more open air at the sides and above. Open each stage (run the game, screenshot with Playwright at desktop and phone-landscape sizes, with the camera pushed to each corner of its bounds) and look for: scenery ending in a visible edge, stretched or tiled-looking backgrounds, empty sky bands, parallax layers that run out, the Void's edge showing through where it used to be hidden. Add real detail where the space now looks empty or unfinished, in each stage's existing style — e.g. more dunes / distant mesas / rock spires for Desert, more skyline, towers, rooftops and signage for City, more cloud banks and sky layers for Asperitas, more sea, horizon, distant islands and sky for Adamaris, more ice cliffs, peaks and aurora for Jökull, more grid for Practice. Respect `reducedMotion`. Keep the stage previews (`js/ui/stage-preview.js`) looking right too.
- `ground.reach` on Adamaris: confirm the continuous ground still runs well past the wider Void.

### 4. Overtime closes in much more, and faster and faster

Overtime should squeeze the arena **noticeably more** than today and accelerate:

- **More closure.** Lower the end gaps in `CONFIG.battle.overtimeVoid` so the final Void is clearly tighter than now (currently 120 past each ledge and 140 below the top), starting from the new, larger margins. Also let the **top** close in during overtime (add a `topEndGap`, e.g. well above the highest jump/platform but far below 1040, and extend `closeVoid` to move the top edge the same way; never below the highest reachable footing plus a safe gap, never past `MIN_VOID_GAP`). Standing on the main stage must always remain safe, as `closeVoid` guarantees today.
- **Accelerating closure.** Replace the linear progress with an easing that starts slow and speeds up continuously to the end — e.g. `pressure = t^k` with `k` around 2–2.5, where `t = overtimeProgress`; put the curve's exponent in `CONFIG.battle.overtimeVoid` (e.g. `closeCurve`). Apply it in one place (where `Battle.update` feeds `setVoidPressure`, or inside `setVoidPressure`), so collision, drawing, the CPU and `launchIsLethal` all keep reading the single `stage.void` in force.
- **Waves accelerate with it.** The drawn waves must visibly speed up progressively as the Void closes. They already scale with `p²`; make them follow the new pressure and raise `maxWaveSpeedMultiplier` if needed so the end of overtime is clearly frantic. Wave speed must change smoothly (no jumps in wave phase — `advanceVoid` integrates speed, keep it that way).
- Online play must stay deterministic: everything stays on the simulation clock (`js/online/lockstep.js`).
- Update `tests/integration/overtime.test.mjs`: closure is monotonic, slower early than late (e.g. less than a quarter of the distance closed by half time with k=2), reaches the new end gaps at t=1, the top edge now closes, the main stage stays safe, and wave speed is non-decreasing.

### 5. The loser is ejected

Whenever the match elects a loser (`finishMatch`, any reason that isn't a draw — clock time-out on points, time-out on Launch Point, overtime points, overtime Launch Point) and the loser is **still in play and visible** (not `lostToVoid`), play the same ejection as a Void fall at their position — the elimination burst in their `eliminationPalette` with its flash, shake and sound — and make them disappear (take them out of play the way `checkVoid`/`detachFromPlay` does, so they're undrawn and untargetable). It's purely presentation and state for the end of the match: it must **not** score a point, count as a fall in match statistics, trigger a respawn, or change the frozen result. A loser already taken by the Void doesn't burst twice. A draw ejects nobody. Make the winner's celebration (`js/game/ai/celebration.js`) work with an absent opponent. Same in Local Play and online. Add tests to `match-score.test.mjs` / `overtime.test.mjs` for each reason.

### 6. Recalculate the fire thresholds

With the Void farther away, 60 / 100 no longer mean "the next hit can end you". Re-derive `LAUNCH_POINT_FIRE`, `LAUNCH_POINT_FIRE_OUT`, `LAUNCH_POINT_INFERNO` and `LAUNCH_POINT_INFERNO_OUT` in `js/ui/hud.js` **by simulation, the same method as the comment there**, against the new geometry (side 468, top 1040, bottom 400, main 1400):

- Burning (tier 1): the Launch Point from which a solid Base Launch 2 hit (3 damage) carries a fighter in the air near the ledge into the Void, and a Base Launch 3 hit does from about a third of the stage — i.e. the next hit is a real danger.
- Inferno (tier 2): the point from which a Base Launch 3 hit ends a life from anywhere on the stage, straight up included.
- Keep the 5-point hysteresis for going out. Write the simulation as a reusable script or test (e.g. under `tests/benchmarks/`) so the numbers can be re-derived later, and rewrite the comment in `hud.js` with the new numbers and what they mean. Check other fighters too, not only Yerma, and pick numbers that hold across the roster.
- Check it against regular-time geometry, not overtime's closing Void (overtime is meant to be more dangerous than the fire says). Update tests that pin the old values, and the docs/spec that mention 60/100.

### 7. CPUs must know all of this

- The CPU already reads `stage.void` live; verify no CPU file caches Void distances, stage length, or hard-codes the old margins or old fire thresholds / "kill percent" assumptions. Search `js/game/ai/` for numeric constants tied to distances to the Void, edge-guard ranges, recovery distances, "safe Launch Point" values and the 60/100 thresholds; retune or derive them from the stage and launch data.
- Their idea of when they or the opponent are in kill range must use the new thresholds (step 6) and the real geometry.
- In overtime they must react to the accelerating, tightening Void, including the new closing top: stay central, avoid high jumps late in overtime, prefer finishing launches as the Void tightens, and anticipate where the edge will be soon (they can read the curve from config, not just the current rectangle).
- Edge-guarding and recovery decisions must account for the longer sides (more room to recover from, longer way back).
- Run `tests/benchmarks/cpu-benchmark.mjs` before and after; regressions in win rate balance, self-destructs (CPU falling into the Void on its own) or match length need fixing. Report the before/after numbers.

